1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
We became national champions —

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,800
with two robots that, honestly,
weren't good enough.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,000
So for the World Championship
we made a decision:

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,500
rebuild two robots from scratch,
much more solid.

5
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:19,900
We're IITA Low Battery Messi,
Salta, Argentina.

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,000
But starting from scratch
had one problem

7
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:29,800
we know well in Argentina:
getting the parts.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,500
Here, importing is slow
and complicated.

9
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:39,000
We couldn't order it all at once,
or from one place:

10
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:43,000
customs forced us
to split the orders.

11
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,900
Parts arrived bit by bit,
over weeks.

12
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,000
So for a long time we had
no complete robot

13
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,000
to test anything physically —
and the deadline kept coming.

14
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:57,900
So how do you move forward
without the robot?

15
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,500
This is the feature
we're proudest of:

16
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:04,900
we tested the robot's BRAIN
without the robot.

17
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,000
The idea is simple: we split
the robot's program in two.

18
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:13,000
On one side, the brain —
where to go, how to kick,

19
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:16,500
the strategy — in pure modules,

20
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:19,800
with nothing tied
to the hardware.

21
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,500
Since that brain needs
no sensors or motors,

22
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:29,000
we run the whole thing
on a laptop.

23
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:32,800
No board, no battery,
no waiting for parts.

24
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:37,000
And this is what we see

25
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,800
when we run all the tests,
right now:

26
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Hundreds of automated tests.
Zero failures.

27
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,500
In seconds,
without touching the robot.

28
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:52,000
Every bug we found,
we turned into a test —

29
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:54,800
so it never happens again.

30
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,000
And the best part:
you can copy it —

31
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,800
especially if parts are
hard to get. Three steps.

32
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:05,000
One: take the decision logic

33
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,500
out of the robot code,

34
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:11,800
into separate modules —
no sensor or motor deps.

35
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,500
Two: keep the test framework

36
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:19,000
inside your own repository,

37
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,800
so it works offline,
with nothing to block it.

38
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:26,500
Three: compile and run the tests

39
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:30,500
on your laptop
with a single command.

40
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:34,800
Change anything, and in seconds
you know if you broke the brain.

41
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,500
When the parts finally arrived,

42
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:45,800
the brain was already tested —
and the robot started to move.

43
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:50,000
All our code is open —
MIT-licensed, on GitHub.

44
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:54,000
If building your robot
is uphill too, take it.

45
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:57,800
See you in Incheon. Thank you!
